From 5e02ffe2da7cc3fdb4681dc2549022b33cc8b86a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: plotarmordev <299844489+plotarmordev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:54:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 35/39] drama: --long-form speaks long inputs in segments that
 hear the ones before

With --long-form, a speech input longer than --long-form-chars (300) is cut at
sentence ends, then clause marks, spaces or anywhere when one sentence is too
long, and packed greedily into segments. Each segment's references are the
request's, the voice's, then up to --long-form-history (1, at most 3) earlier
segments as {their text, the codes they generated}, latest last, the oldest
dropped when the prompt would not fit. The segments' PCM is joined with no
added silence; each seed comes from its own rows. More than 12 segments are
refused; 6000 frames are a budget, and a clipped segment ends the reply with
length. X-Long-Form-Segments gives where each segment starts; streams send the
segments in order with both headers as trailers. Without the flag, or for an
input of one segment, nothing changes.

The fixture gains the reference's rows for a voice plus one and two
carry-overs; a scripted engine tests the joins, history, caps and streams.
